 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: each browser holds a socket to the project's own servers and receives every committed revision as it lands. Subsecond updates are useful but not required; sockets are chosen to give fast updates without a realtime vendor dependency.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed around that choice. The change store keeps replayable event revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably. Delivery manages socket sessions and connection capacity. Browsers reconnect and catch up from their last seen revision, and show connectivity loss rather than presenting a stale board as current.
 
