 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Browsers receive committed revisions over sockets the project hosts. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it adds repeated reads and seconds of lag, against the preference for fast updates. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it adds recurring fees and couples the board to a provider. The accepted cost is operating connection capacity and handling reconnects, carried by the change store (replayable revisions, reliable publication), delivery (session management), and browser sync (reconnect and catch-up).
